          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Issues"} Issues
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#theoretical_issues"} Theoretical issues
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#experimental_issues"} Experimental issues
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (QFT) the term
            %em asymptotic safety
            (
            %a{:href => "#Weinstein79"}> Weinberg 79
            ) refers to the situation where a QFT may not be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizable
            (is not defined on all scales by the choice of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
            of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            has a non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            such that the subspace of the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            whose RG-flow converges to this fixed point is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
          %blockquote
            %p
              One defines the “UV-critical hypersurface” as the set of all those points in the infinite-dimensional theory space which are “pulled” into the fixed point by the inverse RG flow: trajectories lying in this surface approach the fixed point for increasing momentum scales. General arguments and known examples suggest that the UV-critical hypersurface has a finite dimensionality. This dimensionality equals the number of (infrared-) relevant couplings, i.e. couplings which get attracted to the fixed point in the UV. The important point is that once the value of these (few) couplings are known at some scale all other (irrelevant) couplings are fixed by requiring an asymptotically safe theory, that is a trajectory which lies entirely in the UV-critical hypersurface. By this means we achieve that, first, the couplings are determined by a finite number of measurements rendering the theory predictive, and, second, the UV behavior is unproblematic without any unphysical divergences. (
              %a{:href => "#NinkReuter12"}> Nink-Reuter 12, p. 2
              )
          %p
            A key example of a non-renormalizable QFT is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            (
            %a{:href => "#Reuter96"}> Reuter 96
            ).
          %h2#Issues Issues
          %p Issues that the program of asymptotic safety of gravity is facing include the following:
          %h3#theoretical_issues Theoretical issues
          %ol
            %li
              %p All existing computations that see hints for a UV-fixed point do so by first applying a drastic truncation to the space of couplings, and then checking only whether there is a UV-fixed point for the RG-flow in the remaining small subspace. It seems unclear to which extent these approximate considerations may be extrapolated.
            %li
              %p
                Most existing computations consider only pure Einstein gravity without matter coupling. It seems unclear to which extent these results may be extrapolated to the situation where matter is taken account of (but see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#BiemansPlataniaSaueressig17"} Biemans-Platania-Saueressig 17
            %li#DonoghueSummarizes
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Donoghue19"} Donoghue 19
                claims technical problems with the handling of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
                in Newton’s constant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_N
                as currently practiced in the asymptotic safety program:
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    The “
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/running+coupling+constant"}> running
                    ”
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_N(E)
                    comes from power-divergent corrections that vanish in
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional+regularization"} dimensional regularization
                %li
                  %p
                    Computing different
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
                    processes gives different power-divergent corrections: there is no generic common
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_N(E)
                %li
                  %p
                    There is no generic
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi E
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi s
                            %mo ∼
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                            %msup
                              %mi E
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \sim + E^2
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo ∼
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msup
                            %mi E
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \sim -E^2
                    and (unlike in the usual log running) this makes a difference.
            %li
              %p
                If one assumes (as is widely, but no generally believed) that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bekenstein-Hawking+entropy"} Bekenstein-Hawking entropy
                seen in classical gravity is to correspond to a microscopic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
                of its quantum degrees of freedom, then the scaling of this entropy with area as opposed to volume contradicts the assumption that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
                at small scales and higher energies (
                %a{:href => "#Shomer07"}> Shomer 07, section IV
                ) and hence then it contradicts the asymptotic safety of gravity.
              %p
                Similarly, if one trusts the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
                then gravity is fundamentally not a local field theory, only its boundary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
                is, in contradiction with asymptotic safety of gravity (
                %a{:href => "#Shomer07"}> Shomer 07, section IV
                ). But see
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#FR22"} FR22
          %h3#experimental_issues Experimental issues
          %p
            The near criticality of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            (see there at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Higgs+field#MassAndVacuumInstability"} Higgs mass and vacuum (in-)stability
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            of the quartic part of the Higgs potential is close to zero after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            (“RGE”) to around the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            of about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 19
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{19}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            (e.g.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"} BDGGSSS 13, p. 17-18
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticCoupling.png", :width => "400"}/
          %p
            In fact also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            of the quartic coupling
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            (i.e. its logarithmic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            with respect to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            of about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 19
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{19}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            (
            %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"}> BDGGSSS 13, p. 18
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticBetaFunctionRelative.png", :width => "400"}/
          %p
            Earlier it has been suggested that this reflects the principle of asymptotic safety (
            %a{:href => "#ShaposhnikovWetterich09"}> Shaposhnikov-Wetterich 09
            ). But this would mean that not only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-function"} RGE-derivative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            vanish around the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Niedermaier06"} Niedermaier 06, equation (1.5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
            asymptotes to zero. But this does not seem to be the case; in (
            %a{:href => "#BDGGSSS13"}> BDGGSSS 13, p. 17-18
            ) it says:
          %blockquote
            %p
              As shown in fig. 2 (upper right), the corresponding Higgs quartic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-function"} beta-function
              vanishes at a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
              of about
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 17
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{17}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 18
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{18}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
              in units of its pure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+quark"} top quark
              contribution. The vanishing of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_\lambda
              looks more like an accidental cancellation between various large contributions, rather than an asymptotic approach to zero.
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HiggsQuarticBetaFunction.png", :width => "700"}/
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The idea of asymptotic safety as such and as a cure for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Weinstein79
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ultraviolet divergences in quantum theories of gravitation
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/record/159043/"} spire
          %p It gained new popularity with this result:
          %ul
            %li#Reuter96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Reuter"}> Martin Reuter
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Nonperturbative Evolution Equation for Quantum Gravity
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9605030"} arXiv:hep-th/9605030
          %p
            An attempt to conceptually explain why
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            might have a UV-fixed point is in this article:
          %ul
            %li#NinkReuter12
              Andreas Nink,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Reuter"} Martin Reuter
              = succeed "," do
                %em On quantum gravity, Asymptotic Safety, and paramagnetic dominance
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.4325"} arXiv:1212.4325
          %p Observation of a special role of spacetime dimension 2:
          %ul
            %li#LauscherReuter06
              O. Lauscher,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Reuter"} Martin Reuter
              = succeed "," do
                %em Asymptotic Safety in Quantum Einstein Gravity: nonperturbative renormalizability and fractal spacetime structure
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0511260"} arXiv:hep-th/0511260
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7643-7978-0_15"} doi:10.1007/978-3-7643-7978-0_15
          %p
            and speculation of this being related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott+model"} Connes-Lott model
            (see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "2-spectral+triple#Connes06OnRelationToStringVacua"} here
          %ul
            %li#Connes06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              , p. 8 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative Geometry and the standard model with neutrino mixing
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0608226"} arXiv:hep-th/0608226
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Niedermaier06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Niedermaier"}> Max Niedermaier
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Asymptotic Safety Scenario in Quantum Gravity – An Introduction
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0610018"} arXiv:gr-qc/0610018
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Niedermaier"}> Max Niedermaier
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Reuter"} Martin Reuter
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Asymptotic Safety Scenario in Quantum Gravity
                %a{:href => "http://link.springer.com/article/10.12942/lrr-2006-5"} doi:10.12942/lrr-2006-5
            %li
              %p
                Riccardo Martini, Gian Paolo Vacca, Omar Zanusso,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative approaches to non-perturbative quantum gravity
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.13910"} arXiv:2210.13910
            %li
              %p
                Astrid Eichhorn, Marc Schiffer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Asymptotic safety of gravity with matter
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07456"} arXiv:2212.07456
            %li
              %p
                Alexander Bednyakov, Alfiia Mukhaeva,
                %em Perturbative Asymptotic Safety and Its Phenomenological Applications
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.08258"}> arXiv:2309.08258
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Jan M. Pawlowski, Manuel Reichert,
                %em Quantum Gravity from dynamical metric fluctuations
                in
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.10785"}> arXiv:2309.10785
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Critical review:
          %ul
            %li#Shomer07
              %p
                Assaf Shomer,
                %em A pedagogical explanation for the non-renormalizability of gravity
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.3555"}> arXiv:0709.3555
                )
            %li#Donoghue19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Donoghue"}> John Donoghue
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Critique of the Asymptotic Safety Program
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02967"} arXiv:1911.02967
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fphy.2020.00056/full"} doi:10.3389/fphy.2020.00056
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Percacci"}> Roberto Percacci
                ,
                %em Asymptotic Safety FAQs
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.percacci.it/roberto/physics/as/faq.html"}> web
                )
            %li#DonoghuePercacci21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Donoghue"} John Donoghue
                vs.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Percacci"} Roberto Percacci
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Debate on Asymptotically Safe Quantum Gravity
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://pirsa.org/21040021"} video
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pdf.pirsa.org/files/21040021.pdf"} slides
            %li#BiemansPlataniaSaueressig17
              %p
                Jorn Biemans, Alessia Platania, Frank Saueressig,
                %em Renormalization group fixed points of foliated gravity-matter systems
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.06539"}> arXiv:1702.06539
                )
          %p
            The suggestion that asymptotic safety explains the observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+particle"} Higgs particle
            is attributed to
          %ul
            %li#ShaposhnikovWetterich09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Shaposhnikov"}> Mikhail Shaposhnikov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christof+Wetterich"} Christof Wetterich
              = succeed "," do
                %em Asymptotic safety of gravity and the Higgs boson mass
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0208"} arXiv:0912.0208
          %p but see p. 17 of
          %ul
            %li#BDGGSSS13
              Dario Buttazzo, Giuseppe Degrassi, Pier Paolo Giardino,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gian+Giudice"} Gian Giudice
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Strumia"} Alessandro Strumia
              %em Investigating the near-criticality of the Higgs boson
              (
